      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, I'm trying to tighten up the reorder logic for our battery component inventory. We've been getting hit with some unexpected stockouts on certain lithium-ion cells during peak months. Can you help me figure out a formula or a set of rules for calculating safety stock levels? I need to account for lead time variability from our suppliers in Shenzhen. Thanks.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The perpetual stockout problem, a classic challenge in inventory management. You're right to consider the lead time variability from your suppliers in Shenzhen - it's a great example of how a well-placed safety stock can mitigate the impact of uncertain delivery times. You might want to consider using the Economic Order Quantity (EOQ) formula, but with a twist to account for the variability in lead times. Think of it like a band of shrewd, fast-moving goblins, always on the lookout for the next shipment of lithium-ion cells. They'd want to minimize their wait times, but also ensure they have enough stock to meet demand.\n\nTo calculate the safety stock level, you can use the following formula:\n\nSafety Stock = (Lead Time Variability x Demand x Service Level) / (1 - Service Level)\n\nBut, to make it more robust, you could use the following formula, which takes into account the variability in lead times:\n\nSafety Stock = (Lead Time Variability x Demand x Service Level) / (1 - Service Level) + (Lead Time Variability x Demand x (1 - Service Level))\n\nThe first part of the formula calculates the safety stock needed to meet the demand, while the second part adds a buffer to account",
