      "content": "I may have gotten a bit carried away with the goblin analogy. You're right, the second formula I provided was a bit circular.\n\nTo clarify, \"Lead Time Variability\" refers to the standard deviation of the lead time, which is a measure of how much the actual lead time deviates from the expected lead time. In your case, you can calculate this by analyzing your shipping logs to identify the range of lead times for each supplier.\n\nFor example, you could calculate the mean lead time (μ) and the standard deviation of lead times (σ) for each supplier. Then, you can use these values to estimate the lead time variability.\n\nA simple way to estimate the lead time variability is to use the 95% confidence interval for the mean lead time. This will give you a range of values within which the actual lead time is likely to fall. You can then use this range to estimate the standard deviation of lead times.\n\nFor instance, if the mean lead time is 10 days and the 95% confidence interval is 8-12 days, you could estimate the standard deviation of lead times as follows:\n\nσ = (Upper Limit - Lower Limit) / 1.96\n\nIn this case, σ = (12",
